# EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR: (31,15): An array is not an index: expected a Byte, Char, Int or Enum value
# EXPECT_ERROR_COUNT: 8
#~
An index is one value. An array's type is its element's with a dimension, so a
whole Int[] (or Char[], Byte[], an array of an enum) passed the index check as
an Int, and the array's address was used as the index: `values[Indices()]`
with `Indices() ~ Int[]` compiled and failed at run time with "Index out of
bounds: 110744171741680,3". Each of these is refused, once, where the index is
written: a call's result, a variable, a jagged array's row and an array
literal, as the index of a variable and of a call's result. An element of an
array (its own subscript has selected one value) is still an index:
array_index_scalar_forms.obs.
~#
enum Slot {
	First,
	Second
}

class BadIndexWholeArray {
	function : Indices() ~ Int[] {
		return Int->New[2];
	}

	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		values := Int->New[3];
		idx := Int->New[2];
		chars := Char->New[2];
		slots := Slot->New[2];
		rows := Int[]->New[2];
		rows[0] := [0, 1];
		v := values[Indices()];
		v += values[idx];
		v += values[chars];
		v += values[slots];
		v += values[rows[0]];
		v += values[[1, 2]];
		v += Indices()[idx];
		v += Indices()[Indices()];
		v->PrintLine();
	}
}
